          "answer": "**Call emergency services now if your mother has any of these:** chest pain, pressure, or tightness, or pain spreading to the jaw, back, or arm. Heart attack signs can be less obvious, so breathlessness, unusual tiredness, or nausea on their own also matter (red-flag-chest-pain). Also call now for breathlessness at rest or breathlessness that comes on suddenly, being unable to speak in full sentences, blue or grey lips or fingertips, or new drowsiness or confusion (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty). The research summary adds severe or sudden breathlessness especially when lying flat, coughing up pink or frothy fluid, fainting, or swelling that spreads quickly.\n\nThe risk tool labelled this an emergency because of chest pain, but that was from my search wording, not from anything you told me. Please check with her directly. If she has none of the signs above, the swelling alone still needs prompt attention. Her ankle swelling has come back while she has heart failure, so contact her heart team or GP **today** rather than waiting for a routine appointment. Recurring swelling can mean fluid is building up, and her clinicians need to check it. The sources cannot tell how serious it is for her.\n\n**The drug classes:** Guidance I could retrieve says the four foundational heart-failure classes are started together or in quick succession, not added one at a time over months. It applies to reduced ejection fraction, meaning 40% or below. The document I found was only its opening, so I could not confirm which four classes it lists or what each does. Other fragments are partial. A diabetes summary says SGLT-2 inhibitors are preferred in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, though it is a diabetes document. A coronary artery summary says beta-blockers have the strongest long-standing evidence after a heart attack when pumping function is reduced. A hypertension summary says ACE inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers should not be combined. That is a blood-pressure recommendation, so I can't confirm it applies to her. I could not find monitoring recommendations, the strength of evidence for each class, or any points where heart-failure authorities disagree. The heart-failure document had no disagreement section, but I can't say whether none exists.\n\n**What to do next:** Call her heart team today about the swelling. Ask them to review all four medicines, explain what each does, say whether they could be related to the swelling, and set out what monitoring she needs. Do not change, stop, or adjust any medicine, including any water tablets, without speaking to them first.\n\n**Possible causes:** The sources I could retrieve do not explain why ankle swelling happens. The matching tool linked it to heart failure, high blood pressure, osteoarthritis, influenza, and coronary artery disease. These are related topics, not a diagnosis for her. Her clinician is the right person to work out the cause.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
